1
00:00:15,919 --> 00:00:23,600
In many nations, electric power requirements are increasing rapidly.

2
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:34,600
Generation of power from coal, oil, and water resources will continue for many years.

3
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:39,780
But nations faced by high energy costs and other problems are already taking steps to

4
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:45,400
use the new alternative nuclear power generation.

5
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:51,840
Nuclear power reactor technology developed in the United States by the spring of 1958

6
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:58,840
may be useful to many nations.

7
00:01:16,719 --> 00:01:23,540
You're about to see the pressurized water and boiling water reactors, which are already available

8
00:01:23,540 --> 00:01:26,740
for practical electric power generation.

9
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:30,800
And then the homogeneous, organic moderated,

10
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:36,460
sodium graphite, and fast breeder reactors.

11
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:44,740
A major development program is being carried forward to advance further the technology of these

12
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:48,820
and other systems, in order to obtain better fuel use,

13
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:51,320
higher performance and lower costs.

14
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:58,060
Before an experimental power reactor could ever be built

15
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:03,760
a sizable body of fundamental data had to be developed

16
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:06,380
for every aspect of reactor technology.

17
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:13,340
Theoretical work required years

18
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:17,080
and was based on studies by scientists of many nations.

19
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:23,000
High-speed computing machines have been used to calculate flux distribution patterns,

20
00:02:24,580 --> 00:02:31,640
thermal utilization, and interactions under conditions of nuclear reactor operation.

21
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:37,320
Reactor metallurgy requires an investigation of the first magnitude

22
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:41,560
to develop fuels, alloys, and other materials

23
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:45,920
with properties satisfactory for the environment of reactor systems.

24
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:58,080
Special high-flux reactors were built to obtain physics and engineering data on the performance of design models, fuels, and other materials.

25
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:04,080
Tests in these machines reveal the effects of radiation on the properties of materials.

26
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:13,080
Two to three years of testing are required to develop a new material such as an alloy of zirconium,

27
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:17,080
which has the advantages of stainless steel,

28
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:23,080
with only a fraction of its neutron absorption cross-section.

29
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:29,080
The nuclear power and equipment industry

30
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:37,080
is developing facilities for fabricating and testing fuel elements,

31
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:40,080
manufacturing reactor cores,

32
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:46,580
developing and providing a wide variety of components and services.

33
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:52,580
The industry is continuously expanding its facilities.

34
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:06,580
In the United States, the first plant to demonstrate a power reactor concept on an industrial scale

35
00:04:06,580 --> 00:04:12,600
was the Shipping Port Pressurized Water Reactor,

36
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:17,100
built by the Westinghouse Electric Corporation.

37
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:21,900
It has a heat output of 231,000 kilowatts,

38
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:26,280
a steam pressure of 600 pounds per square inch,

39
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:32,560
and the design capacity of 60,000 net electrical kilowatts.

40
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:35,639
Though industrial power will be produced,

41
00:04:35,639 --> 00:04:40,800
The shipping port reactor is essentially an experimental tool to further the development

42
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:45,639
of large-scale power reactors.

43
00:04:45,639 --> 00:04:52,620
The reactor core is a lattice of highly enriched plate-type assemblies surrounded by a blanket

44
00:04:52,620 --> 00:04:56,899
of natural uranium rods.

45
00:04:56,899 --> 00:05:03,500
The primary cycle consists of a single reactor heat source and four individually-shielded

46
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:11,660
coolant loops to circulate the light water moderator coolant. Each loop flows through

47
00:05:11,660 --> 00:05:21,740
a separate heat exchanger with its own steam generator. A pump completes the cycle. One

48
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:28,860
loop can serve as a standby alternate for maintenance. The primary coolant system is

49
00:05:28,860 --> 00:05:33,620
connected to a pressurizing vessel which maintains proper pressure limits in the

50
00:05:33,620 --> 00:05:41,160
reactor. In the secondary plant cycle, low pressure saturated steam enters the

51
00:05:41,160 --> 00:05:48,680
first stage of the turbine, passes to an external moisture separator, re-enters

52
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:56,360
the turbine, and finally exhausts to condensers. Then on to feed water heaters

53
00:05:56,360 --> 00:06:04,020
where the temperature is raised by the exhaust from the separator. A boiler feed

54
00:06:04,020 --> 00:06:11,139
pump completes the circuit. A full-scale working model of the shipping port

55
00:06:11,139 --> 00:06:18,879
reactor was operated for nearly three years at a power level of a few watts to

56
00:06:18,879 --> 00:06:23,100
check analytical calculations and develop some of the reactor physics data

57
00:06:23,100 --> 00:06:31,800
required. Fabricating shipping port components like the 160 ton pressure

58
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:40,080
vessel required major industrial facilities and skills. Machining problems

59
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:47,320
included closer tolerances than had ever been required on parts this large. The

60
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:52,800
scale and precision of the reactors pressure system provided the opportunity

61
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:59,040
for many private industrial firms to gain knowledge and experience with reactor components.

62
00:07:00,879 --> 00:07:06,400
Because of the experimental nature of this reactor, flexibility was a requirement of

63
00:07:06,400 --> 00:07:12,879
many components. The closure head of the pressure vessel has 10 ports to permit

64
00:07:12,879 --> 00:07:20,480
changing or rearranging fuel elements, and others for instrument leads or control rod drives.

65
00:07:20,480 --> 00:07:29,000
Reactor components require material integrity and manufacturing precision of the highest order.

66
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:38,860
Reactor plant containers for the coolant loops and auxiliary equipment were assembled in position,

67
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:45,980
then sealed and tested. The containers are designed to prevent escape of any radioactive

68
00:07:45,980 --> 00:07:52,300
gases or materials that might be accidentally released as a result of major plant casualty.

69
00:07:53,819 --> 00:08:00,540
System components were installed through access openings provided in the pressure containers.

70
00:08:03,980 --> 00:08:08,780
Two steam generators are installed in each of the containers adjacent to the reactor.

71
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:15,180
As a final safety measure, the entire nuclear portion is enclosed in concrete.

72
00:08:15,980 --> 00:08:22,920
The low pressure 1800 rpm turbine and the generator were installed while the nuclear

73
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:28,160
portion of the plant was being completed.

74
00:08:28,160 --> 00:08:34,220
The core was assembled in its cage enclosure at the reactor site.

75
00:08:34,220 --> 00:08:42,980
The 113 blanket sub-assemblies use 14 tons of uranium oxide fuel.

76
00:08:42,980 --> 00:08:46,700
This particular core geometry is expected to yield

77
00:08:46,700 --> 00:08:51,220
about 50% of its power from the seed assemblies

78
00:08:51,220 --> 00:08:59,399
and 50% from the blanket assemblies, including a small amount from the fissioning of plutonium.

79
00:08:59,399 --> 00:09:07,800
Special equipment was developed for precise handling of the core and other components in the reactor vessel.

80
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:17,379
The system is designed to contain the light water primary coolant at 2,000 pounds in order to prevent boiling during steady state operation.

81
00:09:18,899 --> 00:09:25,620
The first core is expected to provide significant data on the questions of long-term reactivity

82
00:09:25,620 --> 00:09:31,539
and fuel life. The entire plant was designed to accommodate a higher powered core in the future.

83
00:09:34,579 --> 00:09:42,599
High purity light water coolant flows upward between the fuel and rods. The heated water flows from the upper part of the

84
00:09:42,599 --> 00:09:48,779
vessel to the intermediate heat exchangers and steam generators. On the

85
00:09:48,779 --> 00:09:55,579
return cycle, a small percentage is bypassed for purification. The reactor is

86
00:09:55,579 --> 00:10:00,099
largely self-regulating due to its negative temperature coefficient, an

87
00:10:00,099 --> 00:10:06,919
inherent control parameter in a system of this design. For example, a drop in

88
00:10:06,919 --> 00:10:14,360
demand for generator load lowers the turbine steam requirement. Hotter water

89
00:10:14,360 --> 00:10:19,919
returns to the steam generator, which means hotter coolant water returning to

90
00:10:19,919 --> 00:10:26,799
the reactor. As the reactor heats up, reactivity is reduced, which in turn

91
00:10:26,799 --> 00:10:33,419
automatically reduces the reactor power level to match the lower load demand.

92
00:10:33,419 --> 00:10:41,099
after extensive testing of components fluid and control systems shipping port

93
00:10:41,099 --> 00:10:49,319
went critical early in December 1957 after additional testing to assure a

94
00:10:49,319 --> 00:10:54,719
proper and safe operation of the reactor and steam plants shipping port went on

95
00:10:54,719 --> 00:11:00,719
the line later that month with 60,000 kilowatts of electrical capacity to take

96
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:06,120
its place in the industrial power network of the Duquesne Light Company.

97
00:11:06,120 --> 00:11:12,340
The reactor is stable and responds almost immediately to load demands.

98
00:11:12,340 --> 00:11:18,840
In the immediate future, shipping ports' contribution to reactor technology will affect its operation

99
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:23,000
more than any requirement for its power.

100
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:28,879
Shipping port will provide valuable experience with heat utilization, system leakage and

101
00:11:28,879 --> 00:11:34,220
maintenance, and other problems. Basic information on technical and economic

102
00:11:34,220 --> 00:11:41,779
questions to benefit the design of future industrial reactors. A further

103
00:11:41,779 --> 00:11:46,460
application of the pressurized water concept is the Army Package Power

104
00:11:46,460 --> 00:11:53,340
Reactor at Fort Belvoir, Virginia. All its basic components can be transported by

105
00:11:53,340 --> 00:12:02,460
air. In full-time service since July 1957, the plant has operated continuously with

106
00:12:02,460 --> 00:12:09,360
no machinery failures. The reactor was shut down temporarily for a routine

107
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:15,600
inspection of the turbine and conventional plant equipment. When

108
00:12:15,600 --> 00:12:21,220
activity in the containment shell has reached background level, the water

109
00:12:21,220 --> 00:12:31,300
filling the excess tube is drained. Inside the containment shell, only a

110
00:12:31,300 --> 00:12:37,779
routine inspection is performed. Penetration of the reactor shield tank

111
00:12:37,779 --> 00:12:40,779
is unnecessary.

112
00:12:45,799 --> 00:12:55,959
Only conventional equipment needs routine maintenance. The nuclear portion

113
00:12:55,959 --> 00:13:01,000
has been so carefully designed that maintenance between fuel changes is not

114
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:10,659
required. After inspection, reactor startup begins. When the reactor is up to

115
00:13:10,659 --> 00:13:16,879
power, the generator is brought online to feed into the Virginia Electric Power

116
00:13:16,879 --> 00:13:23,919
Company network. The reactor automatically increases output to meet

117
00:13:23,920 --> 00:13:33,520
demand. As an additional test of the reliability of the reactor, the load on

118
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:39,380
the generator is dropped instantly from a full 2,000 kilowatts to the station

119
00:13:39,380 --> 00:13:47,040
load of 200 kilowatts. Reactor power level drops to meet the new load and

120
00:13:47,039 --> 00:13:55,039
stabilizes itself. Control rod adjustment is not necessary. The reactor is

121
00:13:55,039 --> 00:14:00,179
presently demonstrating the usefulness and reliability of small capacity

122
00:14:00,179 --> 00:14:08,019
stations. At Indian Point near New York City, the Consolidated Edison Company is

123
00:14:08,019 --> 00:14:12,120
financing the construction of a pressurized water reactor generating

124
00:14:12,120 --> 00:14:22,120
Station. It will serve as a commercial base load plant to produce a part of the

125
00:14:22,120 --> 00:14:27,279
electricity for the network serving the New York City area. The nuclear portion

126
00:14:27,279 --> 00:14:32,700
of the plant is inside a large-span concrete shield and carbon steel

127
00:14:32,700 --> 00:14:39,419
containment shell. The pressure vessel is enclosed in a water shield tank. The core

128
00:14:39,420 --> 00:14:45,200
is an internal thorium thermal converter. The fertile thorium oxide is mixed with

129
00:14:45,200 --> 00:14:53,640
U-235 oxide, formed into pellets, and loaded in rods. As fissionable U-233 is

130
00:14:53,640 --> 00:15:03,220
formed from thorium, it will supplement the U-235 base fuel. Core development is

131
00:15:03,220 --> 00:15:09,440
being performed by the Babcock and Wilcox company at Lynchburg, Virginia.

132
00:15:09,440 --> 00:15:14,600
Major studies with a critical mock-up of the core deal with the behavior of

133
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:24,840
thorium oxide in combination with uranium oxide. The initial fuel loading

134
00:15:24,840 --> 00:15:31,220
for the Indian Point reactor is expected to last 20 months. In-pile irradiation

135
00:15:31,220 --> 00:15:38,220
test loops are being used to investigate fuel element properties, radiation damage and the

136
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:46,440
suitability of fabrication techniques. The schedule for fabrication and delivery of components

137
00:15:48,899 --> 00:15:55,899
is closely coordinated with site construction. Inspection and quality control methods include

138
00:15:55,899 --> 00:16:02,899
and quality control methods include the use of a two million volt X-ray unit.

139
00:16:05,860 --> 00:16:12,860
The reactor will produce 163,000 electrical kilowatts.

140
00:16:13,639 --> 00:16:19,100
Oil fired super heaters are included in the steam circuit to raise the steam temperature

141
00:16:19,100 --> 00:16:29,860
from 450 to 1,000 degrees and increase the plant's net electrical output to 255,000 kilowatts.

142
00:16:29,860 --> 00:16:37,320
The Indian Point Station is scheduled to be operating in 1960.

143
00:16:37,320 --> 00:16:42,659
With research and development assistance from the Atomic Energy Commission, a group of 12

144
00:16:42,659 --> 00:16:49,000
public utilities are financing the Yankee Pressurized Water Reactor Plant, which will

145
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:58,759
produce 134,000 electrical kilowatts at Rowe, Massachusetts.

146
00:16:58,759 --> 00:17:05,839
Major investigations in progress for the Yankee station include hydraulic flow tests with

147
00:17:05,839 --> 00:17:13,279
fuel assembly models, the problems of injecting boron into the reactor coolant stream as a

148
00:17:13,279 --> 00:17:21,319
safety control and the methods for subsequent cleanup by chemical, ultrasonic, or other

149
00:17:21,319 --> 00:17:22,980
means.

150
00:17:22,980 --> 00:17:34,180
The Yankee plant is scheduled for 1960 operation.

151
00:17:34,180 --> 00:17:39,639
The first complete boiling reactor plant in operation is the experimental boiling water

152
00:17:39,640 --> 00:17:48,520
reactor at Argonne National Laboratory. It went critical in December 1956. Although it

153
00:17:48,520 --> 00:17:54,320
was designed for a high degree of experimental flexibility, its initial operation produced

154
00:17:54,320 --> 00:18:01,560
5,000 electrical kilowatts. It regularly supplies a substantial portion of Argonne's daytime

155
00:18:01,559 --> 00:18:10,059
power needs in parallel with other generating plants in a local utility network.

156
00:18:10,059 --> 00:18:16,339
Its reliability compares favorably with conventional stations.

157
00:18:16,339 --> 00:18:21,379
The light water moderator coolant flows by natural convection.

158
00:18:21,379 --> 00:18:28,759
Bulk boiling begins about halfway up the slightly enriched plates in the fuel channels.

159
00:18:28,759 --> 00:18:35,259
An outstanding feature of a boiling water reactor is its inherent safety.

160
00:18:35,259 --> 00:18:42,259
Any sudden increase in reactivity will result in more steam bubble formations, a less dense

161
00:18:42,259 --> 00:18:51,180
moderator, and an immediate stabilization of reactor power at the new level.

162
00:18:51,180 --> 00:18:58,240
Steam at 600 pounds pressure flows through a dryer, directly into the turbine, then back

163
00:18:58,240 --> 00:19:02,480
through a condenser to the reactor.

164
00:19:02,480 --> 00:19:07,059
To compensate for rapid fluctuations in load demand,

165
00:19:07,059 --> 00:19:12,140
an automatic steam bypass valve maintains constant steam

166
00:19:12,140 --> 00:19:17,359
pressure in the reactor system until the control rods

167
00:19:17,359 --> 00:19:21,160
readjust the reactor's power level.

168
00:19:21,160 --> 00:19:25,000
Auxiliary shutdown is provided by a high pressure boric acid

169
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:25,839
injection system.

170
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:34,920
The core structure can accommodate up to 148 fuel assemblies for experimental

171
00:19:34,920 --> 00:19:43,460
variation of core loading. Eight of the fuel elements are of natural uranium. The

172
00:19:43,460 --> 00:19:52,000
other 106 are enriched to 1.44 percent in uranium-235. To provide for

173
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:57,460
achieving at least a 1% burn up, the uranium fuel is alloyed with a small

174
00:19:57,460 --> 00:20:03,519
quantity of zirconium and niobium. The alloy provides strength corrosion

175
00:20:03,519 --> 00:20:09,519
resistance and irradiation stability. During the core loading operation,

176
00:20:09,519 --> 00:20:16,460
neutron flux and density were continuously monitored. After each

177
00:20:16,460 --> 00:20:21,279
loading, there was an attempted approach to criticality. As a safety precaution,

178
00:20:21,279 --> 00:20:26,920
the core vessel was sealed with hold down beams attached to vertical steel

179
00:20:26,920 --> 00:20:33,460
columns. In the unlikely event of an explosion, the beams would direct the

180
00:20:33,460 --> 00:20:39,940
force downward and leave the upper containment shell intact. In its program

181
00:20:39,940 --> 00:20:44,440
of experimental operation, the system has demonstrated its ability to meet

182
00:20:44,440 --> 00:20:51,640
changing load demands with a high degree of stability. The meter on the right

183
00:20:51,640 --> 00:20:57,580
presents total steam flow. The one on the left indicates the amount of steam going

184
00:20:57,580 --> 00:21:02,740
through the bypass valve. The difference in readings is the amount going through

185
00:21:02,740 --> 00:21:09,400
the turbine. No appreciable contamination of the turbine or other components has

186
00:21:09,400 --> 00:21:16,680
been experienced to date. The level is only slightly higher than background.

187
00:21:16,680 --> 00:21:25,680
During reactor operation, routine inspection and maintenance are performed throughout the plant on each shift.

188
00:21:25,680 --> 00:21:34,680
The low pressure system made it possible to use many conventional pumps, valves, and other components.

189
00:21:34,680 --> 00:21:43,680
Leakage problems have been largely eliminated.

190
00:21:43,680 --> 00:21:49,940
For decontamination studies, sodium salts are dissolved and pumped through the system.

191
00:21:51,820 --> 00:21:56,320
Special taps condense steam samples from various points in the system.

192
00:21:58,160 --> 00:22:05,820
These samples give an accurate measurement of how much contamination would be carried into plant components if a fuel element ruptured.

193
00:22:06,840 --> 00:22:13,400
Future tests will include the deliberate rupturing of a fuel element in order to gain more precise information.

194
00:22:13,680 --> 00:22:23,380
One of the basic tests consists of varying reactivity at different frequencies and observing

195
00:22:23,380 --> 00:22:31,600
resultant flux variations. Amplitude changes as frequency changes. The data is analyzed

196
00:22:31,600 --> 00:22:37,440
for fundamental feedback effects.

197
00:22:37,440 --> 00:22:42,440
Most of these studies make it possible to predict stable performance at higher power.

198
00:22:44,440 --> 00:22:50,440
The plant originally produced 5,000 electrical kilowatts from 20,000 kilowatts of heat.

199
00:22:51,440 --> 00:22:57,440
This plant, like the other reactors in the United States, is conservatively designed.

200
00:22:58,440 --> 00:23:04,440
In December 1957, using the original core with no modifications to the system,

201
00:23:04,440 --> 00:23:12,940
reactor power level was increased to 50,000 kilowatts of heat. And in March

202
00:23:12,940 --> 00:23:20,160
1958 power was further increased to 62,000 kilowatts. This can result in a

203
00:23:20,160 --> 00:23:26,480
significant reduction in cost for installed kilowatt. Steam produced in

204
00:23:26,480 --> 00:23:31,940
excess of that needed to generate 5,000 kilowatts of electricity has been dumped

205
00:23:31,940 --> 00:23:42,080
to condensers. Installation of a forced circulation system is planned. Following

206
00:23:42,080 --> 00:23:47,720
this change reactor stability is expected to improve and the core power

207
00:23:47,720 --> 00:23:53,180
density can be increased many fold. Stability of operation with forced

208
00:23:53,180 --> 00:23:59,120
circulation will be compared to that with a natural convection system. The

209
00:23:59,120 --> 00:24:06,120
The boiling water reactor concept is sufficiently advanced now for commercial application.

210
00:24:06,320 --> 00:24:11,640
Future test programs are expected to operate with heavy water and extend the technology

211
00:24:11,640 --> 00:24:18,640
even further in the direction of large, safe, and economical commercial plants.

212
00:24:20,780 --> 00:24:26,540
At Pleasanton, near San Francisco, California, the General Electric Company has financed

213
00:24:26,539 --> 00:24:31,619
and built advanced research and development facilities, including the

214
00:24:31,619 --> 00:24:37,579
Vallecitas Developmental Boiling Water Reactor. Originally designed for an

215
00:24:37,579 --> 00:24:43,639
output of 20,000 thermal kilowatts, the reactor power was increased to 30,000

216
00:24:43,639 --> 00:24:52,019
thermal kilowatts in January 1958. This reactor is a prototype for testing fuel

217
00:24:52,019 --> 00:24:57,579
and operating features of commercial-size reactors such as the

218
00:24:57,579 --> 00:25:06,879
Dresden plant. Its flexibility includes both natural and forced circulation,

219
00:25:06,879 --> 00:25:16,259
single and dual cycle operation. A private utility, the Pacific Gas and

220
00:25:16,259 --> 00:25:21,339
Electric Company, has connected a 5,000 kilowatt generating plant to the

221
00:25:21,339 --> 00:25:30,139
reactor, gaining further experience with direct coupling of a reactor and turbine.

222
00:25:30,139 --> 00:25:38,579
The electricity is fed into the utility's power network.

223
00:25:38,579 --> 00:25:44,859
At Dresden, Illinois, south of Chicago, the first large-sized commercial boiling water

224
00:25:44,859 --> 00:25:50,679
reactor is being constructed by the General Electric Company for the Commonwealth Edison

225
00:25:50,680 --> 00:25:57,960
utility system serving the Chicago area.

226
00:25:58,920 --> 00:26:02,600
Research and development for the project were financed by seven

227
00:26:02,600 --> 00:26:07,480
independent private utility companies and an architect engineer firm.

228
00:26:09,560 --> 00:26:13,480
Dresden will produce 180,000 kilowatts of electricity.

229
00:26:13,480 --> 00:26:24,099
At the Atomic Power Equipment Facility of the General Electric Company, a 60-foot high

230
00:26:24,099 --> 00:26:30,160
test facility incorporating a mock-up of the Dresden Pressure Vessel is used to simulate

231
00:26:30,160 --> 00:26:36,839
various conditions, including heat and water circulation, as well as the refinement of

232
00:26:36,839 --> 00:26:41,740
refueling equipment and procedures.

233
00:26:41,740 --> 00:26:49,460
Here also, fuel element materials and designs are tested for void distribution, steam slip,

234
00:26:49,460 --> 00:26:58,180
steam blanketing, and heat transfer characteristics.

235
00:26:58,180 --> 00:27:07,380
In the main building, fuel for the Dresden core is manufactured on an assembly line basis.

236
00:27:07,380 --> 00:27:14,220
Mod type fuel element fabrication begins in the powder processing room where uranium powder

237
00:27:14,220 --> 00:27:24,280
is mixed with binder and milled, formed into pellets, centered, machined to size, welded

238
00:27:24,280 --> 00:27:30,820
into tubes, inspected, and tested.

239
00:27:30,819 --> 00:27:38,619
After testing the tubes are ready for assembly into bundles and shipment to the reactor site.

240
00:27:44,819 --> 00:27:49,819
Construction of the Dresden station is expected to be completed for pre-operational

241
00:27:49,819 --> 00:27:53,220
testing and criticality in 1959.

242
00:27:55,119 --> 00:27:58,619
Regular operation is scheduled for June of 1960.

243
00:28:00,819 --> 00:28:14,500
In order to avoid the difficulties of fabricating, inserting, removing, and reprocessing solid

244
00:28:14,500 --> 00:28:22,059
fuel elements, which are inherent in heterogeneous reactor systems, a long-range program has

245
00:28:22,059 --> 00:28:27,740
been undertaken to develop reactors which could use a circulating homogeneous fluid

246
00:28:27,740 --> 00:28:35,599
fuel and heat transfer medium to simplify refueling and reprocessing.

247
00:28:35,599 --> 00:28:44,839
However, under reactor operating conditions of radiation, high temperature and pressure,

248
00:28:44,839 --> 00:28:52,640
there have been many problems to overcome, including severe corrosion.

249
00:28:52,640 --> 00:28:58,980
The homogeneous reactor experiment number one, constructed at Oak Ridge National Laboratory

250
00:28:58,980 --> 00:29:08,360
in 1951, was the first experimental power reactor to combine fuel with a moderator coolant.

251
00:29:08,360 --> 00:29:13,500
It was operated as a large-scale engineering and physics experiment.

252
00:29:13,500 --> 00:29:19,860
Highly enriched urinal sulfate, in solution with ordinary water, circulated through a

253
00:29:19,860 --> 00:29:25,940
single region core vessel where the fissile material reached criticality.

254
00:29:25,940 --> 00:29:32,200
This full-scale plastic model of the core was instrumented to study the

255
00:29:32,200 --> 00:29:40,860
hydrodynamics involved at various fluid densities and pressures. Bubbles

256
00:29:40,860 --> 00:29:46,360
simulate the radiolytic gases produced by decomposition of the water and fission

257
00:29:46,360 --> 00:29:54,360
product gases which form in such an aqueous system.

258
00:29:54,360 --> 00:29:59,800
In the first experimental homogeneous reactor, the stainless steel core was surrounded by

259
00:29:59,800 --> 00:30:04,380
a reflector shield of circulating heavy water.

260
00:30:04,380 --> 00:30:08,920
A helium pressurizer absorbed surges from load changes.

261
00:30:08,920 --> 00:30:15,880
In operation, the uranyl sulfate at a pressure of 1000 pounds per square inch left the core

262
00:30:15,880 --> 00:30:19,140
at a temperature of 482 degrees Fahrenheit,

263
00:30:20,220 --> 00:30:26,760
flowed to a heat exchanger, and returned to the core through a canned motor pump.

264
00:30:26,760 --> 00:30:31,200
A steam drum and generator complete the heat utilization circuit.

265
00:30:31,200 --> 00:30:35,260
Reactor power level was controlled by steam demand.

266
00:30:35,260 --> 00:30:39,640
The fissile material was returned to the fuel storage tanks

267
00:30:39,640 --> 00:30:42,200
where fuel concentration was controlled.

268
00:30:42,200 --> 00:30:50,200
Potentially explosive quantities of hydrogen and oxygen gases passed through a flame recombiner,

269
00:30:50,200 --> 00:30:58,200
catalytic recombiner, cold traps, and activated charcoal fission gas absorber.

270
00:30:58,200 --> 00:31:03,200
Harmless waste gases were vented to the atmosphere.

271
00:31:03,200 --> 00:31:10,200
The deuterium cooler, storage tanks and circuits completed the homogeneous reactor system.

272
00:31:10,200 --> 00:31:18,420
Heavy shielding surrounded all components in contact with highly radioactive gases and urinal sulfate fuel solution.

273
00:31:23,200 --> 00:31:31,240
During two years of successful experimental operation, many changes and modifications were made.

274
00:31:31,240 --> 00:31:43,180
Due to the inherently high negative temperature coefficient, nuclear stability was maintained

275
00:31:43,180 --> 00:31:50,039
and power could be increased from 30 watts to 11 megawatts in one second, and returned

276
00:31:50,039 --> 00:31:56,799
to 1 megawatt in one tenth of a second, without damaging the equipment.

277
00:31:56,799 --> 00:32:02,500
However under normal operation, power changes would be undertaken more

278
00:32:02,500 --> 00:32:10,960
gradually. The first homogeneous reactor experiment with its loosely stacked

279
00:32:10,960 --> 00:32:21,339
shielding blocks was dismantled early in 1954. It demonstrated the behavior and

280
00:32:21,340 --> 00:32:29,640
stability of fuel solutions, the ability to attain and maintain leak tightness in a high

281
00:32:29,640 --> 00:32:38,060
pressure system, and safe handling of hydrogen and oxygen produced by radiation decomposition

282
00:32:38,060 --> 00:32:40,860
of the water.

283
00:32:40,860 --> 00:32:46,080
It proved that mechanical control rods are unnecessary and establish direct dependence

284
00:32:46,079 --> 00:32:52,679
of the reactors power on turbine demand.

285
00:32:52,679 --> 00:32:59,019
A second homogeneous reactor experiment with a two-region reactor has been constructed

286
00:32:59,019 --> 00:33:05,619
and operated to test engineering feasibility and advance the technology of the homogeneous

287
00:33:05,619 --> 00:33:08,299
reactor concept.

288
00:33:08,299 --> 00:33:14,740
The principle of operation is basically the same as that in the first homogeneous reactor

289
00:33:14,740 --> 00:33:22,059
the addition of an integrated chemical processing plant.

290
00:33:22,059 --> 00:33:28,200
A cooling coil surrounds the carbon steel blast shield, which contains the double region

291
00:33:28,200 --> 00:33:38,160
reactor, a carbon steel pressure vessel lined with stainless steel, and the zirconium alloy

292
00:33:38,160 --> 00:33:40,980
core vessel.

293
00:33:40,980 --> 00:33:48,759
rods are not necessary for this system. In operation, fully enriched urinal sulfate in

294
00:33:48,759 --> 00:33:57,220
heavy water solution passes through the core at 2,000 pounds pressure. Diffuser screens

295
00:33:57,220 --> 00:34:06,360
provide a smooth expansion of flow. During initial operation, heavy water will circulate

296
00:34:06,360 --> 00:34:13,500
through the blankets. With some modification fertile materials may be introduced to demonstrate

297
00:34:13,500 --> 00:34:19,800
a breeding cycle.

298
00:34:19,800 --> 00:34:28,360
The inlet or diffuser section was formed of plate. The first section has a 30 degree angle.

299
00:34:28,360 --> 00:34:36,039
Perforated plates are arranged in the conical section to distribute the inlet fluid uniformly.

300
00:34:36,039 --> 00:34:44,699
The top half of the tank is a hemisphere.

301
00:34:44,699 --> 00:34:51,679
Oxygen free welding of the curved surfaces required a number of equipment modifications.

302
00:34:51,679 --> 00:34:58,980
The completed core vessel and associated components for handling radioactive fluids are located

303
00:34:58,980 --> 00:35:05,940
in the main reactor cell which will be covered with concrete shielding slabs.

304
00:35:05,940 --> 00:35:11,280
The shielding assembly also provides a barrier against the escape of any gases which might

305
00:35:11,280 --> 00:35:18,740
result from a system leak.

306
00:35:18,740 --> 00:35:24,760
Chemical processing of reactor fluid takes place in an adjacent shielded cell.

307
00:35:24,760 --> 00:35:31,000
It is controlled from a separate control room.

308
00:35:31,000 --> 00:35:44,000
The system is brought to operating pressure and temperature with no uranium in the system.

309
00:35:44,000 --> 00:35:51,599
Then the uranyl sulfate solution is injected to bring the reactor up to criticality concentration.

310
00:35:51,599 --> 00:35:58,039
Average core temperature in the reactor is controlled by varying the amount of uranium.

311
00:35:58,039 --> 00:36:04,019
The power level automatically adjusts itself to the load demand.

312
00:36:04,019 --> 00:36:08,659
This reactor has encountered various difficulties which have caused delays.

313
00:36:08,659 --> 00:36:15,800
However, the reactor achieved criticality in December 1957.

314
00:36:15,800 --> 00:36:22,420
In April 1958, shortly after its operating power level had been raised to its full design

315
00:36:22,420 --> 00:36:30,579
power of 5000 thermal kilowatts the reactor was shut down because instruments indicated

316
00:36:30,579 --> 00:36:35,740
that fuel from the main core system was transferring to the blanket heavy water

317
00:36:35,740 --> 00:36:45,000
system further work proceeds to overcome these difficulties concurrent studies are

318
00:36:45,000 --> 00:36:50,599
underway to develop a thorium oxide slurry for breeding use in the blanket region

319
00:36:52,420 --> 00:37:00,420
Manufacturing processes being developed include the production of thorium oxide particles having desired properties.

320
00:37:02,420 --> 00:37:11,420
Using different concentrations, the viscosity and engineering performance of various slurries are measured.

321
00:37:11,420 --> 00:37:26,639
A blanket system mock-up similar to this model is used to study the behavior of slurry particles

322
00:37:26,639 --> 00:37:33,320
under different system temperatures, pressures, and velocities.

323
00:37:33,320 --> 00:37:38,740
It is expected that present problems will be overcome and that homogeneous reactors

324
00:37:38,740 --> 00:37:48,620
will be able to attain their potential.

325
00:37:48,620 --> 00:37:55,300
At the National Reactor Testing Station, the Organic Moderated Reactor Experiment, conducted

326
00:37:55,300 --> 00:38:01,880
for the Atomic Energy Commission by Atomics International, went critical for the first

327
00:38:01,880 --> 00:38:09,840
time in September 1957.

328
00:38:09,840 --> 00:38:16,260
Operating temperatures will range from 500 to 700 degrees Fahrenheit at a system pressure

329
00:38:16,260 --> 00:38:23,059
of 200 pounds.

330
00:38:23,059 --> 00:38:30,500
Standard hardware and construction materials are used throughout the system.

331
00:38:30,500 --> 00:38:34,440
at high temperatures and low pressures.

332
00:38:34,440 --> 00:38:42,159
This reactor is serving as a testing laboratory to study characteristics of the commercially

333
00:38:42,159 --> 00:38:52,320
available terphenols being used as the moderator, reflector, and coolant.

334
00:38:52,320 --> 00:39:02,100
The organic material is melted, then fed through an expansion tank into the reactor.

335
00:39:02,100 --> 00:39:09,760
The coolant flows up from the bottom plenum between the plate-type fuel elements to the

336
00:39:09,760 --> 00:39:16,760
pool, which forms a biological shielding above the core of the reactor, then on to an air

337
00:39:16,760 --> 00:39:20,200
blast heat exchanger.

338
00:39:20,200 --> 00:39:24,160
A continuous purge of nitrogen over the surface of the organic coolant

339
00:39:24,160 --> 00:39:28,340
prevents buildup of hydrogen and light hydrocarbon gases.

340
00:39:29,540 --> 00:39:35,380
These decomposition gases are diluted, then exhausted through a stack.

341
00:39:35,720 --> 00:39:38,640
The organic is slowly damaged by irradiation.

342
00:39:40,060 --> 00:39:46,100
Small quantities are bypassed to a still, which separates the high-boiling materials thus formed

343
00:39:46,100 --> 00:39:49,120
and returns the organic to the main system.

344
00:39:49,119 --> 00:39:56,500
waste is pumped to a storage tank. Test instrumentation provides information on

345
00:39:56,500 --> 00:40:01,420
the operation of the reactor, heat transfer properties and heat rejection

346
00:40:01,420 --> 00:40:08,079
rates, as well as behavior of the organic under the varying conditions expected of

347
00:40:08,079 --> 00:40:13,239
a power reactor. Measurement of surface temperature of the fuel plates can

348
00:40:13,240 --> 00:40:19,340
detect a build-up of a polymer film from decomposition of the organic.

349
00:40:19,340 --> 00:40:24,880
Techniques for removing the film, if it should form, are being developed.

350
00:40:24,880 --> 00:40:30,380
Sample taps are provided to obtain specimens of the coolant and gases from various points

351
00:40:30,380 --> 00:40:37,060
in the system for laboratory evaluation of the effects of the reactor environment on

352
00:40:37,060 --> 00:40:41,840
decomposition and damage.

353
00:40:41,840 --> 00:40:49,559
The organic moderated reactor experiment is advancing the technology of hydrocarbon use

354
00:40:49,559 --> 00:40:55,680
by establishing data which will be required in the design of power reactors using this

355
00:40:55,680 --> 00:41:06,300
high temperature, low pressure concept.

356
00:41:06,300 --> 00:41:12,980
An experimental reactor has been built and operated to evaluate the feasibility of molten

357
00:41:12,980 --> 00:41:18,840
sodium as a reactor coolant and heat transfer medium.

358
00:41:18,840 --> 00:41:25,160
The sodium-cooled reactor concept affords high steam temperature and pressure conditions

359
00:41:25,160 --> 00:41:31,300
similar to those of modern fossil fuel burning plants.

360
00:41:31,300 --> 00:41:38,920
Enrichment fuel is used in a graphite moderator. The experiments and sodium

361
00:41:38,920 --> 00:41:43,900
reactor technology are conducted and partially financed by Atomics

362
00:41:43,900 --> 00:41:51,460
International at Santa Susana near Los Angeles, California. Major studies deal

363
00:41:51,460 --> 00:41:57,640
with the life of fuel control and safety elements in a system which can produce

364
00:41:57,639 --> 00:42:08,639
produce 20,000 kilowatts of heat with a pressure of only three pounds at the free sodium surface.

365
00:42:08,639 --> 00:42:17,639
The coolant is highly radioactive as it leaves the reactor at 960 degrees Fahrenheit.

366
00:42:17,639 --> 00:42:23,639
Heat is transferred to a non-radioactive secondary sodium loop and steam generator,

367
00:42:23,639 --> 00:42:27,659
which feeds a 7,500 kilowatt turbo generator.

368
00:42:27,659 --> 00:42:30,920
A similar auxiliary circuit,

369
00:42:30,920 --> 00:42:35,099
rated at one megawatt, is operated simultaneously

370
00:42:35,099 --> 00:42:39,440
to provide emergency removal of fission product decay heat.

371
00:42:39,440 --> 00:42:44,819
To remove contaminants in each loop, a small percentage of sodium

372
00:42:44,819 --> 00:42:51,819
is circulated through hot and cold traps. In the sodium reactor core,

373
00:42:51,820 --> 00:42:59,120
the graphite moderator is enclosed in hexagonal zirconium cans. Channels

374
00:42:59,120 --> 00:43:04,000
through the center of each moderator assembly permit location of the fuel

375
00:43:04,000 --> 00:43:10,200
elements in triangular patterns. Additional element channels are provided

376
00:43:10,200 --> 00:43:18,100
at corner locations for controls, safety rods, the neutron source, and experimental

377
00:43:18,099 --> 00:43:26,619
assemblies. Each fuel element consists of a cluster of seven stainless steel clad

378
00:43:26,619 --> 00:43:33,739
tubes filled with the slightly enriched metallic uranium slugs. The column of

379
00:43:33,739 --> 00:43:38,799
slugs is thermally bonded to the inside of the tube with a sodium potassium alloy

380
00:43:38,799 --> 00:43:45,400
for uniform transfer of heat from the fuel. To demonstrate the feasibility of

381
00:43:45,400 --> 00:43:51,639
coupling a power plant to a sodium system. A single pass sodium to water

382
00:43:51,639 --> 00:43:58,400
steam generator was developed. It is enclosed in a stainless steel shell to

383
00:43:58,400 --> 00:44:06,220
reduce the likelihood of a leak and a reaction between sodium and water. The

384
00:44:06,220 --> 00:44:12,619
sodium flows past double wall tubes. Pressurized mercury acts as a heat

385
00:44:12,619 --> 00:44:19,019
transfer medium and monitors for tube failure as water flashes to steam in the inner tube.

386
00:44:22,059 --> 00:44:26,940
The generator plant was developed and is being operated entirely at the expense of

387
00:44:26,940 --> 00:44:34,779
a private utility, the Southern California Edison Company. Overall operation and maintenance

388
00:44:34,779 --> 00:44:39,500
of the sodium graphite reactor experiment have been encouragingly successful.

389
00:44:42,619 --> 00:44:55,579
On December 20th and 21st, 1951, the experimental breeder reactor number one produced electricity

390
00:44:55,579 --> 00:45:00,339
from nuclear energy for the first time in the world.

391
00:45:00,339 --> 00:45:07,019
This reactor was designed for maximum experimental flexibility to demonstrate the feasibility

392
00:45:07,019 --> 00:45:11,920
of breathing in a fast neutron power reactor plant.

393
00:45:11,920 --> 00:45:19,420
During the first four years of operation, two experimental cores were used.

394
00:45:19,420 --> 00:45:26,240
The reactor arrangement for these cores consisted of highly enriched fuel rods, a hexagonal

395
00:45:26,240 --> 00:45:31,840
stainless steel separator, and an inner breeding blanket of natural uranium rods.

396
00:45:31,840 --> 00:45:36,700
The inner blanket served as part of the reflector geometry.

397
00:45:36,700 --> 00:45:44,480
Air-cooled uranium bricks clad with stainless steel formed the outer breeding blanket.

398
00:45:44,480 --> 00:45:50,080
Sodium-potassium coolant flowed in series down through the inner breeding blanket, then

399
00:45:50,080 --> 00:45:53,460
up through the core.

400
00:45:53,460 --> 00:45:57,260
Coolant circulates through a heat exchanger at low pressure.

401
00:45:57,260 --> 00:46:04,160
A non-radioactive sodium-potassium loop transfers the heat to boiling water in a second heat

402
00:46:04,159 --> 00:46:16,039
exchanger. These heat exchanger tubes are nickel diffusion bonded to copper with

403
00:46:16,039 --> 00:46:21,079
longitudinally machined holes for leak detection gas. Sodium-potassium

404
00:46:21,079 --> 00:46:30,839
circulates outside the tube. Water flashes to steam inside. An inert

405
00:46:30,840 --> 00:46:35,940
atmosphere is maintained above all free surfaces to prevent coolant contact with

406
00:46:35,940 --> 00:46:41,720
air. A cold trap and convection cooling loop for shutdown of the main system

407
00:46:41,720 --> 00:46:50,100
complete the circuit. Liquid metal cooling systems were studied at

408
00:46:50,100 --> 00:46:58,019
temperatures ranging from room temperature to 650 degrees Fahrenheit. A

409
00:46:58,019 --> 00:47:02,599
A conversion ratio of 1 was attained.

410
00:47:03,460 --> 00:47:08,820
Other experiments exposed various materials in the reactor's high energy,

411
00:47:08,820 --> 00:47:16,119
high flux region. In normal operation, the plant was quite stable, reliable, and

412
00:47:16,119 --> 00:47:23,780
largely self-regulating. When severe operating conditions were imposed, such

413
00:47:23,780 --> 00:47:33,220
as high power at reduced coolant flow, an oscillation in power occurred. On November

414
00:47:33,220 --> 00:47:40,800
29, 1955, an overshoot in power occurred during an experiment in which the coolant flow was

415
00:47:40,800 --> 00:47:48,220
deliberately stopped and some safety devices bypassed. This resulted in a partial meltdown

416
00:47:48,220 --> 00:47:56,200
of the core. The core was removed and sent to Argonne National Laboratory for

417
00:47:56,200 --> 00:48:04,900
disassembly in the inert atmosphere of a hot cell. The molten fuel and uranium

418
00:48:04,900 --> 00:48:13,720
which had alloyed with iron from the jackets was removed. All enriched fuel

419
00:48:13,720 --> 00:48:21,340
was recovered and reprocessed. The meltdown experience provided valuable

420
00:48:21,340 --> 00:48:29,840
information on certain safety aspects of fast reactors. A third core was designed

421
00:48:29,840 --> 00:48:38,380
to investigate the effect of fuel element bowing on reactor stability. With

422
00:48:38,380 --> 00:48:43,780
this Mark III core, bowing can be eliminated or permitted to occur to a

423
00:48:43,780 --> 00:48:54,220
certain degree. Stability under both conditions will be studied. Valves have

424
00:48:54,220 --> 00:48:58,059
been installed to permit series or parallel flow conditions for the

425
00:48:58,059 --> 00:49:03,519
coolant. In parallel, blanket flow can be adjusted to have a temperature rise

426
00:49:03,519 --> 00:49:11,699
equal to that in the core. The third core is highly instrumented to detect thermal

427
00:49:11,699 --> 00:49:17,679
expansion and reactivity changes. In April 1958 the reactor was brought to a

428
00:49:17,679 --> 00:49:24,940
power level of 930 thermal kilowatts. It has already demonstrated the breathing

429
00:49:24,940 --> 00:49:30,880
principle and excellent stability under induced oscillations and other extreme

430
00:49:30,880 --> 00:49:36,119
testing conditions. The experience is being incorporated in the design of a

431
00:49:36,119 --> 00:49:40,900
larger fast reactor now under construction to further advance

432
00:49:40,900 --> 00:49:46,800
practical power generation with breeder reactors. The second experimental reactor

433
00:49:46,800 --> 00:49:52,720
will produce 16,500 net electrical kilowatts and is expected to be

434
00:49:52,720 --> 00:49:58,060
operating in 1960.

435
00:49:58,060 --> 00:50:04,380
Near Detroit, Michigan, the Power Reactor Development Company, with associated electric

436
00:50:04,380 --> 00:50:13,660
utilities and industrial firms, is constructing the Enrico Fermi atomic power plant to demonstrate

437
00:50:13,660 --> 00:50:21,620
the fast reactor concept on a commercial scale.

438
00:50:21,619 --> 00:50:32,799
is proceeding on major components such as the refueling device, pumps, piping, and the

439
00:50:32,799 --> 00:50:39,779
reactor vessel. Initially, the Detroit Edison Company plans to utilize the Fermi plant's

440
00:50:39,779 --> 00:50:50,779
heat output of 300,000 kilowatts to produce 100,000 kilowatts of electricity. After sufficient

441
00:50:51,619 --> 00:50:57,299
experience at this level, production can be increased to the generators capacity

442
00:50:57,299 --> 00:51:10,960
of 150,000 kilowatts. Completion is scheduled for 1960. In this film you have

443
00:51:10,960 --> 00:51:16,719
seen illustrations of work undertaken in the United States by the spring of 1958

444
00:51:16,720 --> 00:51:21,740
to make pressurized water and boiling water reactors available

445
00:51:21,740 --> 00:51:28,160
and to develop improved technology for homogeneous, organic moderated,

446
00:51:29,200 --> 00:51:35,740
sodium graphite, and fast breeder reactors, which may lead to lower costs.

447
00:51:36,500 --> 00:51:41,740
The United States will continue major efforts to advance nuclear technology

448
00:51:41,740 --> 00:51:45,760
to help meet the needs of the United States.

449
00:51:46,720 --> 00:51:49,840
and the other nations of the world.

450
00:51:49,840 --> 00:51:54,060
Many significant advances will be made by other nations

451
00:51:54,060 --> 00:51:56,800
through their own independent efforts.

452
00:51:56,800 --> 00:52:02,220
But to cooperate with other nations and with their professional people,

453
00:52:02,220 --> 00:52:05,780
research groups, industries, and governments,

454
00:52:05,780 --> 00:52:11,980
the United States will continue to make available technical information, technical assistance,

455
00:52:11,980 --> 00:52:15,120
training, fuel, and other materials,

456
00:52:15,119 --> 00:52:20,199
